  "summary": "A mixed-flow pump adds energy partly by centrifugal action and partly by the lift of its blades: liquid leaves the impeller diagonally, between the radial direction of a centrifugal pump and the axial direction of a propeller pump. This suits large flows at moderate heads (specific speed roughly between radial and axial machines). In the vertical wet-pit form shown, the bowl hangs in the intake sump and a discharge elbow above the floor turns the flow into the outlet pipe.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Rotodynamic, mixed flow",
    "configuration": "Vertical wet pit, single stage, line-shaft",
    "api_610_type": "VS1 / VS3-style arrangement"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "impeller": "Enclosed mixed-flow, 4 blades",
    "shaft_seal": "Gland packing in elbow stuffing box",
    "specific_speed_note": "Mixed-flow pumps typically sit around Ns ≈ 4,000–9,000 (US units) / nq ≈ 80–160; verify against design data."
  },
